{
  "dom_id": "step-q236468",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The force $F,$ measured in newtons $(\\text{N})$, needed to extend a spring by some length $l,$ measured in $\\text{m},$ is directly proportional to $l.$ When the extension of the spring is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force on the spring is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ What is the constant of variation?\n\nThe constant of variation is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{N/m}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the extension $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF=kl,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the extension is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =k\\cdot (0.005) \\\\ 25 & =0.005k \\\\ \\frac{25}{0.005} & =k \\\\ 5\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{F}{l},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}} & =\\text{N/m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=5\\,000\\,\\text{N/m}.$",
